    64	Introduction
    65	The geometry of affine toric surfaces still attracts the researches1. Every affine toric surface
    66	over C except for A1∗ × A1∗ and A1 × A1∗ , where A1∗ = A1 \ {0}, is the quotient of A2 by a small
    67	finite cyclic subgroup G ⊆ GL(2, C).2
    68	Throughout the paper, by ‘acyclic curve’ we mean a connected and simply connected
    69	complex affine algebraic curve. A classification of acyclic curves on the affine plane, both
    70	irreducible and reducible, up to the action of the automorphism group of the plane is well
    71	known, see e.g. [1], [37], [47], [53], [55]; we recall it in subsection 1.1 below. In section 3 we
    72	classify acyclic curves on affine toric surfaces. Similarly as in [54], actions of one-parameter
    73	groups play a crucial role in this classification.
    74	Let π : A2 → X = A2 / G be the quotient morphism, and let C be an irreducible acyclic
    75	curve on X. Then π ∗ (C) is an acyclic (reducible and non-reduced, in general) curve on
    76	A2 . We show (see theorems 3.2 and 3.5) that applying an appropriate automorphism of the
    77	affine plane we can transform the curve π ∗ (C) and the G-action on A2 to canonical forms
    78	simultaneously.
    79	In subsection 2.2, given an acyclic plane curve C, we describe the stabilizer subgroup
    80	Stab(C) ⊆ Aut(A2 ) of all automorphisms which preserve C. We use this description in
    81	subsections 3.1 and 3.2 in order to obtain the canonical forms of irreducible acyclic curves
    82	on an affine toric surface X. We treat separately the cases of the curves passing or do not
    83	passing through the singular point of X. This leads in subsection 3.3 to the conclusion that
    84	every irreducible acyclic curve on X is the closure of a non-closed orbit of a Gm -action on X.
    85	Furthermore, if such a curve is contained in the smooth locus Xreg then it is smooth and is as
    86	well an orbit of a Ga -action on X, hence is included in a one-parameter family of such curves.
    87	We show that any affine toric surface X possesses only finitely many equivalence classes of
    88	embedded affine lines (see theorem 3.9). This is an analog of the celebrated Abhyankar-MohSuzuki Embedding Theorem, which says that there is just one class of embeddings of the
    89	affine line in the affine plane (see theorem 1.1 below).
    90	The above description enables us to classify in subsection 3.4 all reduced simply connected
    91	curves on an affine toric surface, whenever they are irreducible or not.
    92	Section 4 is devoted to the automorphism groups of affine toric surfaces. In theorem 4.2 we
    93	obtain an analog of the classical Jung-van der Kulk theorem on a free amalgamated product
    94	structure on the group Aut(A2 ) (see theorem 1.4). Using this theorem we describe in theorems
    95	4.15 and 4.17 (reductive) algebraic groups acting effectively on affine toric surfaces.
